# Friendly names for WireGuard peers in Dashmon.
#
# Copy this file into Dashmon's data directory, next to wireguard.dump, then
# edit it. The script deploy/wireguard-dump.sh reads it and replaces each peer's
# public key with the name below before writing the dump.
#
# In container mode (default), copy it straight into the container:
#
#   curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/supersouris7/dashmon/main/deploy/wireguard-names.conf.example \
#     | sudo docker exec -i dashmon sh -c 'cat > /app/data/wireguard-names.conf'
#
# Then edit it in place:
#
#   sudo docker exec -it dashmon vi /app/data/wireguard-names.conf
#
# The file path can be changed with $DASHMON_WIREGUARD_NAMES (a path inside the
# container in container mode, a path on the host in host-file mode).
#
# Format: one line per peer, the public key then the name. Separate key and name
# with spaces; the name may contain spaces. The public key is the base64 string
# printed by `wg show all dump` (the second column of a peer line, or the
# "public key:" line of `wg show`). Copy it verbatim.
#
#   <public-key> <name>
#
# Blank lines and lines starting with # are ignored. A peer with no entry keeps
# showing as its public key.

AbCdEf0123456789AbCdEf0123456789AbCdEf0123456789X= Office NAS
ZzYyXx9876543210ZzYyXx9876543210ZzYyXx9876543210Y= Phone
